<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.6.0: Lesson Overview</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02176</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.6.0: Lesson Overview</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b6c0eacc-7cfc-46b7-8e13-235de61ffc79</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>In this lesson, you will begin to rewrite quadratic expressions from standard to factored form. You will look at expressions of the form \((x + m)(x + n)\) and \((x − m)(x − n)\) where \(m\) and \(n\) are positive.</para>
<para>When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Explain how the numbers in a quadratic expression in factored form relate to the numbers in an equivalent expression in standard form. </item>
  <item> Rewrite a quadratic expression in standard form when given the quadratic expression in factored form. </item>
  <item> Rewrite a quadratic expression in factored form when given the quadratic expression in the form of \(x^2+ bx + c\). </item>
</list>
<para>Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 8.6.1: Finding and Reasoning Unknown Factors </item>
  <item> 8.6.2: Using Diagrams to Understand Equivalent Expressions </item>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent">
    <item> 8.6.2: Self Check </item>
    <item> 8.6.2: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 8.6.3: Rewriting Quadratic Expressions in Standard Form </item>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent">
    <item> 8.6.3: Self Check </item>
    <item> 8.6.3: Additional Resources </item>
  </list>
  <item> 8.6.4: Finding the Missing Numbers in Pairs of Equivalent Expressions </item>
</list>
<para>After that, you’ll <emphasis>practice and review</emphasis>.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> 8.6.5: Practice </item>
  <item> 8.6.6: Lesson Summary </item>
</list></section>
